The cascade tests cover three shapes deliberately: a target in the middle of the queue, a target that is already the oldest ticket, and an unknown target ticket. Together with the eligibility and volume-normalization tests, this is ten checks that exercise the pure logic "RequestClose()" and "ExecuteSingleClose()" are built on—not those two methods themselves, which only a live or demo account can actually exercise.
Backtesting Notes
The Strategy Tester will exercise the pyramiding logic and the cascade decision-making correctly on a hedging-mode test account—which prerequisite closes in which order, under which mode. What it will not do, in a typical backtest, is actually return "TRADE_RETCODE_FIFO_CLOSE" from the simulated trade server, since that rejection is specific to the real trade servers of brokers that enforce the rule, nor does a clean backtest exercise the full range of real-world retcodes "ExecuteSingleClose()" now distinguishes—a requote, a frozen level, a "DONE_PARTIAL" fill, and the rest are conditions a generic simulated server does not generate. Treat a clean backtest as confirmation that the guard's decision logic behaves correctly. Do not treat it as confirmation of behavior against a real server. That confirmation requires a demo or live account, where "AccountRequiresFifo()" and, if it occurs, "IsFifoConfirmedByRejection()" reflect the account's actual behavior.
Run on a hedging-mode account ("ACCOUNT_MARGIN_MODE_RETAIL_HEDGING") to actually see multiple tickets accumulate; a netting-mode test will still complete without errors, but every position on the symbol collapses to one ticket and there is nothing for the cascade logic to demonstrate.
Known Limitations
Scoping "Refresh()" to the EA's own magic number is a safety boundary against the guard ever closing a position it does not own—not a complete guarantee of server-side FIFO correctness. If other positions exist on the same symbol outside this EA's magic number—opened manually, or by a different EA—the trade server's own FIFO rule still considers them, and this guard has no visibility into them at all. A ticket this guard believes is FIFO-eligible among its own tickets can still be rejected by the server if an older, foreign position exists on the same symbol; "ExecuteSingleClose()" retcode handling is what catches that when it happens, not something "Refresh()" filtering prevents in advance. Whether to extend this to an account-wide queue that looks past magic number entirely—and accepts the ownership risk that comes with cascading through positions this guard did not open—is a design decision deliberately left to the reader rather than defaulted into this code.
Whether a broker's FIFO enforcement actually spans both directions on a symbol together, or scopes separately by direction, is not documented by MQL5 either way; "FIFO_SCOPE_SYMBOL_WIDE" is this article's default assumption, not a confirmed fact about any specific broker, and "FIFO_SCOPE_PER_DIRECTION" exists specifically so that assumption can be changed rather than silently relied on.
Auto-cascade mode closes every prerequisite ticket in full before acting on the target. This is not equivalent to closing the target alone—it realizes the P&L, ends the holding period, and changes the risk distribution of every ticket it closes along the way, which is a different outcome from the single-ticket action the original request named, not a larger version of the same one. Confirming that is the intended tradeoff, for a given strategy, matters more than the mechanics of how the cascade executes.
"AccountRequiresFifo()" is the documented, authoritative signal for whether an account enforces this rule, and should be checked before relying on anything else in this article. "IsFifoConfirmedByRejection()" is weaker and secondary: it reports only that the server has, at some point during this run, actually rejected a request with retcode 10045—direct evidence the enforcement is live, but its absence proves nothing, since it may simply mean no request has yet been misordered against a server that would have rejected it.
The related "no simultaneous opposite-direction positions" constraint, sometimes bundled with FIFO under the same regulatory umbrella, is out of scope here. That rule is typically enforced by the server itself rejecting or netting an opposing order at submission time, rather than something a close-side compliance layer manages.
"Refresh()" rescans every open position on the symbol on every call, including once per cascade step now that each step re-verifies before executing. This is deliberately simple and correct for the position counts a pyramiding strategy realistically produces, but it is not the right choice for an account managing hundreds of simultaneous tickets on one symbol.
Re-verifying immediately before each cascade step narrows the race window between building the cascade and executing it, but does not eliminate it—the position set can still change in the instant between that re-check and the trade call itself. The guard's abort-on-first-failure behavior keeps that scenario safe rather than silently wrong, but it does not retry automatically; a caller that needs guaranteed eventual completion should re-issue the request after a failure rather than assume one call is sufficient.
Conclusion
TRADE_RETCODE_FIFO_CLOSE is the rejection code returned when a trade server detects a FIFO violation, while ACCOUNT_FIFO_CLOSE allows an EA to check whether the account requires FIFO compliance before submitting a close request.
In this article, we implemented a FIFO-compliant position management layer consisting of two components. CFifoPositionQueue provides deterministic ordering of the EA's positions by opening time, with ticket numbers used to resolve ties. CFifoComplianceGuard translates a strategy's close request into FIFO-aware execution, either rejecting an out-of-order request in strict mode or closing prerequisite positions in sequence in auto-cascade mode. The guard rechecks position ordering during execution and evaluates actual server return codes rather than assuming that a submitted request succeeded.
Unit tests validate the ordering, eligibility, cascade construction, and volume normalization logic. However, the implementation is limited to positions belonging to the EA, while broker FIFO restrictions may also apply to positions opened manually or by other EAs. Cascade execution stops at the first failure, and broker-specific behavior must be verified on an appropriate demo or live account.
The resulting architecture separates trading decisions from execution constraints, allowing strategies to express which position they intend to close while a dedicated layer handles FIFO compliance. This makes position management more predictable, testable, and easier to diagnose without hiding the limitations imposed by the trading server.
